{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"Draw near to the altar,\" and \"Aaron drew near to the altar and slaughtered\"</b> — in my opinion, according to the plain sense, it means: draw near to the north side of the altar and perform there the sin-offering and the burnt-offering, for their slaughter is on the north side. And Moses said this briefly, for Aaron already knew this.\n\nBut in Torat Kohanim (Shemini Millu'im ch. 8) our Rabbis raised a question about this and offered a parable. To what may the matter be compared? To a king of flesh and blood who married a woman, and she was bashful before him. Her sister entered to her and said: \"My sister, why did you enter into this matter — is it not to serve the king? Pluck up your courage and come serve the king.\" So Moses said to Aaron: \"Aaron my brother, why were you chosen to be High Priest — is it not to minister before the Place (HaMakom)? Pluck up your courage and come perform your service.\"\n\nAnd some say: Aaron saw the altar in the form of a bull, and he was afraid of it. Moses entered to him and said: \"Aaron my brother, do not fear that which you fear. Pluck up your courage and come draw near to it.\" Therefore it says \"Draw near to the altar\" and \"he drew near to the altar\" — with alacrity.\n\nAnd the reason for this matter is that because Aaron was holy to YHWH and there was no sin in his soul other than the deed of the calf, that sin was fixed in his thought, as it says (Psalms 51:5), \"and my sin is before me continually,\" and it appeared to him as though the form of the calf was there, hindering his atonements. Therefore he said to him: pluck up your courage — lest you be so lowly in spirit, for Elohim had already accepted his deeds.\n\nAnd others explain that the adversary (satan) was showing him this, as they said there: \"Aaron my brother, even though the Place (HaMakom) has been reconciled to atone for your iniquities, you must put something into the mouth of the adversary (satan), lest he accuse you upon your entry into the sanctuary,\" etc. — in Torat Kohanim (Shemini Millu'im ch. 3).\n\nAnd the reason for \"and make atonement for yourself and for the people\": it means, draw near to the altar to perform all the offerings, and first perform your sin-offering and your burnt-offering, and make atonement for yourself first through your offerings and for the people afterward, when you offer the people's offering and make atonement for them through their offering. This teaches that one who is innocent may come and make atonement for one who is liable (Yoma 43a)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:07.226448545Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}